## Vercel Web Analytics Installation Complete

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js App Router project.

### What was implemented:

1. **Installed @vercel/analytics package**
   - Added `@vercel/analytics` as a dependency using npm
   - Package version installed: Latest version from npm registry

2. **Modified src/app/layout.tsx**
   - Added import: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next';`
   - Added `<Analytics />` component at the end of the `<body>` tag
   - Placement follows official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router
   - Analytics component placed after the ThemeProvider to ensure proper initialization

### Implementation details:

- **Framework**: Next.js 16.3.3 with App Router
- **Package Manager**: npm (as indicated by package-lock.json)
- **Installation Method**: Followed official Vercel Web Analytics quickstart guide from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
- **Framework-specific setup**: Used `@vercel/analytics/next` import as recommended for Next.js App Router

### Files modified:
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added Analytics import and component

### Verification performed:
✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✅ Linter ran with no new errors introduced in modified files
✅ TypeScript compilation passed
✅ No test suite found in project (no tests to run)

### Next steps for the user:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section)
3. Once deployed, analytics data will begin appearing in the Vercel dashboard after users visit the site

### Notes:
- The Analytics component is lightweight and only activates when deployed to Vercel
- No additional configuration is required
- The component automatically tracks page views and web vitals
- Pre-existing linting errors in other components were not addressed as they are outside the scope of this change
